    <activity>
        <title>Quadrant Classification Challenge </title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Work in small groups to practise identifying which quadrant an angle belongs to using a large circle drawn on the ground or on manilla paper.
            </p>
            <p>
                The teacher or students prepare folded angle cards with secret values. Each card is folded in half so the angle value is hidden, and the students pick a card from a central pile and place it in the quadrant they think is correct.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Draw a large circle on the ground or on a sheet of manilla paper and divide it into four equal quadrants.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Label the quadrants I, II, III and IV, and write the sign of cosine and sine for each quadrant.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Prepare a set of folded angle cards. Each card should show one angle on the inside, for example:
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>30°</li>
                            <li>120°</li>
                            <li>-45°</li>
                            <li>210°</li>
                            <li>315°</li>
                            <li>-150°</li>
                            <li>270°</li>
                            <li>135°</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Place the folded cards in the centre of the circle so the angle values are hidden. Students take turns picking a card, unfolding it only after deciding, and then placing it in the quadrant they believe contains that angle.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            After all cards have been placed, open each card and check whether the classification is correct. If any card has been placed in the wrong quadrant, move it to the correct quadrant and discuss why.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Complete the table below for each angle by recording the quadrant and the signs of cosine, sine and tangent in that quadrant.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <p>
                    <table>
                    <title>Angle Quadrant Classification</title>
                    <tabular halign="left" border="minor" left="medium" right="medium" top="medium" bottom="medium">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Angle</cell>
                            <cell>Quadrant</cell>
                            <cell>Cosine sign</cell>
                            <cell>Sine sign</cell>
                            <cell>Tangent sign</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>30°</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>120°</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>-45°</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>210°</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>315°</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table> 
                </p>         
    </activity>

    <exploration>
        <p>
            Use the interactive below to drag the point around the unit circle. Pay attention to how the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-coordinates of the point change as the angle <m>\theta</m> (measured from the positive <m>x</m>-axis) increases.
        </p>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-unit-circle" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="2:1" source="jsxgraph/unit-circle.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-unit-circle" surface="jsxboard" aspect="2:1"/>
        </interactive>
    </exploration>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To connect the unit circle equation to the trigonometric ratios <m>\sin</m> and <m>\cos</m>, and to derive the Pythagorean Identity.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Anchor the discussion in the equation <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m> that Learners derived in the activity. Introduce <m>\cos\theta</m> and <m>\sin\theta</m> as labels for the coordinates Learners have already met<mdash/>not as new objects.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A common mistake is to read <m>\cos^2\theta</m> as <m>\cos(\theta^2)</m>. Stress that it means <m>(\cos\theta)^2</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </axiom>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>From the Unit Circle to Sine and Cosine</title>
        <p>
            Consider any point <m>P = (x, y)</m> on the unit circle in the first quadrant, and let <m>\theta</m> be the angle that the radius <m>OP</m> makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis. Dropping a perpendicular from <m>P</m> to the <m>x</m>-axis forms a right triangle whose hypotenuse is <m>1</m>, whose side adjacent to <m>\theta</m> has length <m>x</m>, and whose opposite side has length <m>y</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Applying SOH-CAH-TOA in this triangle:
            <md>
                <mrow>\cos\theta = \frac{\text{adjacent}}{\text{hypotenuse}} = \frac{x}{1} = x</mrow>
                <mrow>\sin\theta = \frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{hypotenuse}} = \frac{y}{1} = y</mrow>
            </md>
            So the coordinates of any point on the unit circle at angle <m>\theta</m> are simply <m>(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Substituting <m>x = \cos\theta</m> and <m>y = \sin\theta</m> into the unit-circle equation <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m> gives
            <md><mrow>\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta = 1.</mrow></md>
            This relationship<mdash/>called the <term>Pythagorean Identity</term><mdash/>holds for <em>every</em> angle <m>\theta</m>, and is one of the most useful identities in trigonometry.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <insight>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>unit circle</term> is the circle of radius <m>1</m> centred at the origin. Its equation is
                        <md><mrow>x^2 + y^2 = 1.</mrow></md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        For an acute angle <m>\theta</m> measured from the positive <m>x</m>-axis, the point on the unit circle at angle <m>\theta</m> has coordinates
                        <md><mrow>(x, y) = (\cos\theta, \sin\theta).</mrow></md>
                        That is, the <m>x</m>-coordinate <em>is</em> the cosine of the angle, and the <m>y</m>-coordinate <em>is</em> the sine.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Substituting these into the equation of the unit circle gives the <term>Pythagorean Identity</term>:
                        <md><mrow>\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta = 1.</mrow></md>
                        Here, <m>\cos^2\theta</m> is shorthand for <m>(\cos\theta)^2</m>, and similarly for <m>\sin^2\theta</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Reading Sine and Cosine off the Unit Circle</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The point <m>P = \left(\dfrac{1}{2}, \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)</m> lies on the unit circle. Let <m>\theta</m> be the (acute) angle that the radius <m>OP</m> makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis. Find <m>\cos\theta</m> and <m>\sin\theta</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
            Since <m>P</m> lies on the unit circle, its coordinates are <m>(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)</m>. Comparing with the given coordinates:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\cos\theta = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\sin\theta = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                (You may recognise this as the point corresponding to <m>\theta = 60°</m>.)
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->


